 serb  has been a lifeline and showed what was  possible  and despite the serb racialized and  disadvantaged communities have been  impacted especially hard  the pandemic exposing the inequities  that already  existed it goes without saying that  living in poverty  is very expensive dr nahid dasani is a  palliative care physician who  specializes  in caring for homeless and vulnerable  populations  i do think that basic income and  policies  associated with it have the potential to  improve health outcomes but also reduce  health care utilization like a stay in a  hospital  or a visit to an emergency department  for a fuller picture of what basic  income could do  you have to head a little west and back  in time nearly 40 years  in 1974 the small town of dauphin  manitoba became the test case for  something dubbed  mincom researchers wanted to see if  people stopped working  after you gave them a basic income the  model was simple  people qualified if they made under a  certain amount of money  for example a family of four was  eligible  if their total income was under thirty  eight hundred dollars a year or  just over nineteen thousand in twenty  twenty dollars  health economist evelyn farget studied  the program data and found those who  were employed  stayed employed only two groups stopped  working  mothers who extended their paltry  maternity leave  and young men those teens didn't just  collect the cash and do nothing  forget says it bought them the ability  to continue their education  well if you think about the differences  in opportunities that are available  for somebody who finished grade 12 in  1975 for example as opposed to somebody  who left high school at age 16 and got  an immediate job you can imagine the  different  life path they would follow over the  next 40 years advocates believe the  candidate emergency response benefit or  serb is a big reason why  basic income is finding traction now  serb paid out  81.6 billion dollars and helped nearly  9 million canadians but that benefit is  not available to all those who need it  basic income would be the parliamentary  budget officer has pegged the cost of a  basic income scheme  at anywhere between 47 and 98  billion dollars over six months  opponents insist it's too expensive  and the return on investment is just not  there  what do you say to people who believe  that a guaranteed income or a basic  income  will just see more people sit at home  and do nothing and collect that money  my first answer is that there is  absolutely no evidence of that  in any of the pilots and any of the  programs that we've run  sheila regier heads up the basic income  canada network one of many groups that's  been promoting the idea for decades  she sees the pandemic as the perfect  time to push  for basic income again people want to do  better  and all of the pilots verify that  proponents say  politicians have to stop looking at  guaranteed income  as an expenditure and more like an  investment  we're investing in the kind of society  we want to build we're investing in  opportunities for our children and for  our grandchildren  and we're looking for returns on that  investment over the longer run  and i think it's very hard for somebody  who's looking to  fight an election in two years or three  years or four years to make those kinds  of long-term  decisions but those are the kinds of  decisions we have to make i think as a  society  measuring societal success can be  difficult but in the manitoba income  project  hospital use decreased by 8.5 percent  which suggests health care savings  across the country  could be significant in the more recent  ontario project mcmaster university  researchers found an  overwhelming majority of participants  reported their health was somewhat or  much  better than before basic income  political will  and potential costs have prevented a  livable income from becoming a  countrywide reality but this time there  is  a cross-party effort to push ahead so we  held our own town hall with three mps  from different parts of the country  all with the same goal by show of hands  who wants some form of livable or basic  income to be put into place for  canadians i put two hands up  oh yes yes jennica atwin is the green mp  for fredericton leah gazan is the ndp mp  for winnipeg center  and naderskin smith is the liberal mp  for toronto beaches east york  i think we need to be bold now we cannot  wait we cannot wait for our suicide  rates to continue to increase  we cannot wait for the disabled  community to continue to sound the alarm  that they're consistently forgotten  forgotten about  it needs to be in addition to a current  and future uh government  uh programs and supports things like  um you know medicare affordable  accessible social housing we need  investments and nader skin smith  is leading a movement within the liberal  caucus to make basic income  a top priority let's do what we can over  the coming six months  as the crb is still in place to develop  plans to address  income and wealth inequality erskine  smith points out basic income programs  already exist  in two forms seniors with the guaranteed  income supplement  and old age security pension the canada  child benefit  is also there for families but he admits  there is a missing middle  of working canadians who have been left  behind  seeing the cross partisan political will  gives jessie gollum hope  something she didn't have much of a few  years ago  and she has a message for prime minister  justin trudeau  as he guides canada out of the pandemic  i think if there's any country that can  do this like we have a chance to  not only build a better world and lead  an economic revolution we have a chance  to lead something on a world  stage  next week on the new reality the impact  of covet 19 on the brain  could be conceived of us accelerating  brain aging  i didn't know who i was where i was i  don't know how long i was like that i  just remember coming out of it and still  feeling really really confused running a  bath is a treacherous thing to do now  because  i have to sit there and watch it because  i don't know if i leave the room if i'm  going to remember that that's what i was  doing  the virus might impact the brain in  certain people impairing their  activities of daily living so this is a  healthcare issue and a societal issue  [Music]  that is the new reality for this week  i'm donna friesen  thanks for watching  you
 

